lessons.body:
**Codegen: record to get started**

```bash
npx playwright codegen http://localhost:3000
```

This opens a browser and an inspector. As you click and type, Playwright writes the test code, choosing role and label locators where it can. Treat the output as a draft. It is excellent for learning locator syntax and for quickly mapping a flow. Then clean it up: give the test a behaviour name, replace anything fragile, and add real assertions, because recorded tests usually check little.

**Trace viewer: debugging a failure**

A trace is a recording of a test run: every action, the page snapshot before and after each step, network calls and console output. With `trace: 'on-first-retry'` in the config, a failed-then-retried test saves one. Open it with:

```bash
npx playwright show-trace test-results/shifts-request-chromium-retry1/trace.zip
```

Or open the HTML report and click the trace for the failed test. In the viewer you step through the timeline, see exactly what the page looked like when the action failed, and read the locator that did not match. Most "why did it fail in the pipeline" questions are answered here: element not found, covered by a dialog, wrong text, or a network error.

You can also debug locally with `npx playwright test --debug`, which opens the inspector and steps through the test.

**Logging in once with storage state**

Most apps need a login. Logging in through the UI in every test is slow. Instead, log in once in a setup step, save the browser's cookies and storage to a file, and reuse it.

```ts
// tests/auth.setup.ts
import { test as setup, expect } from '@playwright/test';

const authFile = 'playwright/.auth/dispatcher.json';

setup('log in as dispatcher', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('/login');
  await page.getByLabel('Email').fill(process.env.TEST_USER!);
  await page.getByLabel('Password').fill(process.env.TEST_PASSWORD!);
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Sign in' }).click();
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/\/shifts/);
  await page.context().storageState({ path: authFile });
});
```

```ts
// playwright.config.ts (projects section)
projects: [
  { name: 'setup', testMatch: /.*\.setup\.ts/ },
  {
    name: 'chromium',
    use: { ...devices['Desktop Chrome'], storageState: 'playwright/.auth/dispatcher.json' },
    dependencies: ['setup'],
  },
],
```

Add `playwright/.auth` to `.gitignore`: the file contains session data. Credentials come from environment variables or pipeline secrets, never from the repository, and use a dedicated test account.

Two patterns remove repeated code from tests. Both are optional at a small scale; know what they are for.

**Page object**

A page object is a class that holds the locators and common actions for one page. Playwright's own guide describes it as giving each part of a web app its own class. Here is one for the TodoMVC demo:

```ts
export class TodoPage {
  readonly input: Locator;
  readonly items: Locator;
  readonly count: Locator;

  constructor(readonly page: Page) {
    this.input = page.getByPlaceholder('What needs to be done?');
    this.items = page.getByTestId('todo-item');
    this.count = page.getByTestId('todo-count');
  }

  async goto() { await this.page.goto('./'); }
  async add(title: string) {
    await this.input.fill(title);
    await this.input.press('Enter');
  }
  async complete(title: string) {
    await this.items.filter({ hasText: title }).getByRole('checkbox').check();
  }
}
```

If the placeholder changes, you edit one line, not twenty tests.

**Fixture that builds it**

A fixture creates the object, hands it to the test, and cleans up. `test.extend` adds one:

```ts
const test = base.extend<{ todoPage: TodoPage }>({
  todoPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    const todoPage = new TodoPage(page);
    await todoPage.goto();
    await use(todoPage);       // the test body runs here
  },
});

test('add and complete', async ({ todoPage }) => {
  await todoPage.add('Call Maple Street Pharmacy');
  await todoPage.add('Confirm shift 1042');
  await todoPage.complete('Confirm shift 1042');
  await expect(todoPage.items).toHaveCount(2);
  await expect(todoPage.count).toContainText('1 item left');
});
```

I ran this test and it passes. Playwright's docs note that fixtures run only for tests that ask for them, unlike a `beforeEach` that runs for every test in the file.

**Keep assertions in the test**

A common rule: the page object finds things and does things; the test decides what is correct. `expect` calls stay in the test file so a failure reads as a requirement.

**When not to bother**

Under about ten tests on two pages, plain locators in the tests are fine. Add a page object when the same locators repeat in three or more places.

